{"meta":{"chapter":"01","section":"02","title":"Rigid Body Mechanics in Motion","course":"dynamics","description":"Model translation and rotation together, interpret torque, and connect moment-arm geometry to engineering design decisions.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"dynamics-01-02-lo-01","statement":"Calculate torque at rotational joints"},{"id":"dynamics-01-02-lo-02","statement":"Understand moment arms and angular velocity"},{"id":"dynamics-01-02-lo-03","statement":"Apply standard kinematic formulas to complex bodies"}],"concept_ids":["rigid_body","torque","angular_velocity","kinematics","joints"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"prereq_section_ids":["dynamics/01/01"],"practice_ids":["curvilinear_concept_1","curvilinear_concept_2","curvilinear_numeric_1","curvilinear_numeric_2","curvilinear_circular","dynamics_01_02_lo_01_direct_check","dynamics_01_02_lo_03_direct_check"],"lo_practice_map":{"dynamics-01-02-lo-01":["dynamics_01_02_lo_01_direct_check"],"dynamics-01-02-lo-02":["curvilinear_circular","curvilinear_concept_1","curvilinear_concept_2","curvilinear_numeric_1","curvilinear_numeric_2"],"dynamics-01-02-lo-03":["dynamics_01_02_lo_03_direct_check"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Rigid Body Mechanics in Motion\n\n<figure-block id=\"section-anchor-dynamics-01-02\" caption=\"Learning map for Rigid Body Mechanics in Motion: principle, evidence, and transfer.\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/section-anchors/dynamics/01-02.svg\" alt=\"Learning map for Rigid Body Mechanics in Motion, connecting rigid body, torque, angular velocity to evidence and transfer.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n\n\n> **\"Give me a lever long enough and a fulcrum on which to place it, and I shall move the world.\"**  \n> - Archimedes\n\n---\n\nOn the morning of June 1, 2009, investigators studying a swing-stage scaffold collapse in New York traced the failure to a single overlooked quantity: the turning effect of an off-center load on the support frame. The platform itself was strong enough to carry the weight. What it could not survive was the **moment** that weight produced once the load shifted toward one end. The platform did not just sink; it rotated, and rotation is exactly the behavior a particle model can never predict. The moment a body can spin as well as slide, force magnitude alone stops telling the whole story. We need geometry too.\n\nWhen we treated an object like a particle, we only cared about where its mass center moved. Real engineering systems are richer than that. A robot leg swings, a wrench rotates, a satellite tumbles, and a bicycle wheel both translates and spins. To model those systems, we need the idea of a **rigid body**: an idealization in which the distance between any two points stays constant even while the object moves. This assumption lets us track both **translation** and **rotation** without having to model every molecule in the object.\n\n---\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nBy the end of this section, you will be able to:\n\n1. **Define** a rigid body and explain why orientation, not just position, must be tracked\n2. **Relate** angular quantities to linear quantities using $v = r\\omega$ and $a_t = r\\alpha$\n3. **Calculate** torque from a force, a moment arm, and an included angle\n4. **Apply** moment-arm geometry to real joint and mechanism design decisions\n5. **Resolve** the acceleration of a point on a rotating body into tangential and normal parts\n\n---\n\n## Why Rigid Bodies Matter\n\nA rigid-body model becomes useful whenever shape and orientation affect performance.\n\n- A door opens because the force is applied away from the hinge.\n- A prosthetic knee needs enough joint torque to support body weight while still moving smoothly.\n- A drone propeller blade can have the same center-of-mass motion as another blade but a completely different rotational speed and aerodynamic response.\n\nIn other words, a rigid body asks two questions at once:\n\n1. Where is the object moving?\n2. How is the object rotating while it moves?\n\n> **Key Insight:** A particle has three things you can ask about (its position, velocity, acceleration). A planar rigid body adds three more (its angle $\\theta$, angular velocity $\\omega$, and angular acceleration $\\alpha$). Dynamics is largely the discipline of keeping those two stories consistent.\n\n---\n\n## Translation Versus Rotation\n\nEvery rigid-body motion can be interpreted as some combination of:\n\n- **Translation:** every point in the body moves with the same velocity.\n- **Rotation:** points in the body move around an axis, so points farther from the axis sweep faster.\n\nThat second point is the key reason rotation feels different from straight-line motion. If a wheel doubles its angular speed, the rim experiences a much larger linear speed than the hub because:\n\n$$\nv = r\\omega\n$$\n\nwhere $v$ is tangential speed, $r$ is distance from the axis, and $\\omega$ is angular velocity (in rad/s).\n\nIf the angular speed changes, the tangential acceleration follows:\n\n$$\na_t = r\\alpha\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is angular acceleration (in rad/s$^2$).\n\nThere is a second acceleration hiding here. Even at constant angular speed, a point on the rim is constantly changing direction, so it carries a **normal (centripetal) acceleration** directed toward the axis:\n\n$$\na_n = \\frac{v^2}{r} = r\\omega^2\n$$\n\nThe total acceleration of a point on a rotating body is the vector sum of these perpendicular parts:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{a = \\sqrt{a_t^2 + a_n^2}}\n$$\n\n> **Critical Check:** A common error is to report only $a_t$ and forget $a_n$. At high spin rates the normal term usually dominates by orders of magnitude, which is why flywheels and turbine rotors fail by bursting outward, not by twisting.\n\n---\n\n## Torque: The Rotational Version of Push\n\nA force does not automatically create rotation. Rotation depends on both the force and **where** that force is applied relative to the pivot or axis. That turning effect is **torque** (also called a moment):\n\n$$\n\\vec{\\tau} = \\vec{r} \\times \\vec{F}\n$$\n\nFor magnitude in a planar problem:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{|\\tau| = rF\\sin{\\theta}}\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the distance from the pivot to the point of application, $F$ is the applied force, and $\\theta$ is the angle between the position vector $\\vec{r}$ and the force $\\vec{F}$.\n\nThe factor $r\\sin\\theta$ has its own name: the **perpendicular moment arm** $d$. It is the shortest distance from the pivot to the line of action of the force. Writing $\\tau = Fd$ is often faster than tracking the angle, and it makes the geometry obvious: torque is large when the force pushes far from the pivot and perpendicular to the arm. This equation explains why a longer wrench feels stronger. The force from your hand may be the same, but the larger moment arm creates more torque.\n\n### Sign Convention\n\nIn planar problems we pick a positive sense (usually counterclockwise) and sign every torque accordingly. The net torque is the algebraic sum:\n\n$$\n\\sum \\tau = \\sum \\tau_{\\text{CCW}} - \\sum \\tau_{\\text{CW}}\n$$\n\nUse the explorer to feel the geometry of $\\tau = rF\\sin\\theta$. Hold the force and lever length fixed and sweep the angle: torque peaks at $90^\\circ$ and vanishes when the pull lines up with the handle. The explorer evaluates $\\sin$ in radians, so the angle is converted from degrees with the factor $\\pi/180 \\approx 0.017453$.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Torque from a force at an angle (tau = r F sin theta)\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"F\",\"label\":\"Applied force F\",\"min\":0,\"max\":300,\"step\":5,\"unit\":\"N\",\"default\":120},{\"name\":\"r\",\"label\":\"Lever length r\",\"min\":0.05,\"max\":0.6,\"step\":0.01,\"unit\":\"m\",\"default\":0.3},{\"name\":\"theta\",\"label\":\"Angle between r and F\",\"min\":0,\"max\":180,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"deg\",\"default\":90}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Moment arm d = r sin theta\",\"unit\":\"m\",\"expr\":\"r * sin(theta * 0.0174533)\"},{\"label\":\"Torque tau = r F sin theta\",\"unit\":\"N m\",\"expr\":\"r * F * sin(theta * 0.0174533)\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"theta\",\"output\":1}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\n<inline-check\n  question=\"In the torque explorer, at which angle between the lever and the force is the torque the LARGEST for fixed r and F?\"\n  conceptId=\"torque\"\n  explanation=\"Torque is r F sin(theta), and sin(theta) reaches its maximum of 1 at 90 degrees. A perpendicular pull delivers the full moment arm d = r; any other angle wastes part of the force pushing along the handle.\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"At 0 degrees (force along the handle).\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"At 45 degrees.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"At 90 degrees (force perpendicular to the handle).\",\"correct\":true}]'></inline-check>\n\n---\n\n## Worked Examples\n\n### Example 2.1: Torque From a Wrench at an Angle\n\n**Problem:** A mechanic pulls on a $0.30 \\text{ m}$ wrench with a force of $120 \\text{ N}$. Find the torque about the bolt (a) when the pull is perpendicular to the handle and (b) when the pull is at $60^\\circ$ to the handle.\n\n**Step 1: Draw the situation.** On the FBD, place the pivot at the bolt, draw $\\vec{r}$ along the handle, and draw $\\vec{F}$ at its tip. The angle $\\theta$ is measured between $\\vec{r}$ and $\\vec{F}$.\n\n**Step 2: Perpendicular pull ($\\theta = 90^\\circ$).**\n\n$$\n\\tau = rF\\sin 90^\\circ = (0.30)(120)(1) = \\boxed{36.0 \\text{ N m}}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Angled pull ($\\theta = 60^\\circ$).**\n\n$$\n\\tau = rF\\sin 60^\\circ = (0.30)(120)(0.8660) = \\boxed{31.18 \\text{ N m}}\n$$\n\nThe angled pull wastes part of the force pushing along the handle, where it cannot turn the bolt. The lesson generalizes: maximum torque always comes from a force applied perpendicular to the moment arm.\n\n---\n\n### Example 2.2: Moment-Arm Design for an Exoskeleton Knee\n\n**Problem:** An exoskeleton knee actuator can apply a force of $180 \\text{ N}$ perpendicular to a short lever attached to the joint. Compare the available joint torque when the attachment point sits $0.04 \\text{ m}$ from the joint center versus $0.06 \\text{ m}$.\n\n**Step 1: Apply $\\tau = Fd$ at the first attachment point.**\n\n$$\n\\tau_1 = (0.04)(180) = \\boxed{7.2 \\text{ N m}}\n$$\n\n**Step 2: Move the attachment outward.**\n\n$$\n\\tau_2 = (0.06)(180) = \\boxed{10.8 \\text{ N m}}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Interpret.** The percentage gain is\n\n$$\n\\frac{10.8 - 7.2}{7.2} \\times 100\\% = 50\\%\n$$\n\nThe second design produces 50% more torque without changing the motor. The tradeoff is that the larger mechanism may be bulkier, slower, or harder to package, because a longer arm sweeps a wider arc for the same joint angle. This is exactly the sort of design compromise rigid-body mechanics helps us reason about.\n\nWork the same compromise as a designer would. The team needs more stand-up torque from the knee but cannot grow the package indefinitely. Trace a decision path and see where the geometry pushes back.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Sizing the exoskeleton knee actuator\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"Bench tests show the current knee gives 7.2 N m (180 N at a 0.04 m arm) but users need about 10 N m to stand from a chair. What do you change first?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Lengthen the moment arm to 0.06 m\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"Geometry first: tau = F d, so a 50% longer arm gives 50% more torque for the same motor.\"},{\"label\":\"Specify a larger, stronger motor\",\"next\":\"n3\",\"feedback\":\"More force works, but it adds mass, current draw, and cost before you have exhausted free geometry.\"},{\"label\":\"Apply the force closer to the joint center\",\"next\":\"n4\",\"feedback\":\"Moving the force toward the pivot SHRINKS the moment arm, so torque drops. Wrong direction.\"}]},\"n2\":{\"prompt\":\"The 0.06 m arm hits 10.8 N m, clearing the target. What is the cost of the longer arm?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"It sweeps a wider arc for the same joint angle, so the mechanism is bulkier and may be slower\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. A longer lever buys torque but eats packaging space and angular speed.\"},{\"label\":\"There is no cost; torque rose for free\",\"next\":\"n6\",\"feedback\":\"Not quite. Every torque gain from geometry trades against package size, swing clearance, or joint speed.\"}]},\"n3\":{\"prompt\":\"A bigger motor raises force to 250 N at the original 0.04 m arm, giving 10 N m. What did this cost you that the geometry route did not?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Added battery drain and mass on a wearable device\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Force-side fixes raise power and weight, which matters a lot on the body.\"}]},\"n4\":{\"prompt\":\"Torque has fallen below 7.2 N m and the user cannot stand. How do you recover?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Move the attachment back outward to lengthen the arm\",\"next\":\"n2\",\"feedback\":\"Good recovery: restore the moment arm, the variable tau is most sensitive to.\"}]},\"n5\":{\"prompt\":\"You meet the 10 N m target. What is the rigid-body lesson?\",\"feedback\":\"tau = F d couples force and geometry: lengthening the moment arm is the cheapest torque gain, but it trades against package size, swing clearance, and joint speed. Good design balances both knobs against the wearable constraints.\",\"choices\":[]},\"n6\":{\"prompt\":\"Reconsider the swing geometry.\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"A longer arm does carry a packaging and speed penalty\",\"next\":\"n5\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly. The torque gain is real, but never free.\"}]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n---\n\n### Example 2.3: A Point on a Spinning Wheel\n\n**Problem:** A grinding wheel of radius $0.35 \\text{ m}$ accelerates uniformly from rest to $600 \\text{ rpm}$ in $4.0 \\text{ s}$. At the final speed, find the rim's tangential speed, its normal acceleration, and the total acceleration of a rim point at the instant it reaches full speed (where $\\alpha$ is still acting).\n\n**Step 1: Convert to consistent units.**\n\n$$\n\\omega = 600 \\ \\frac{\\text{rev}}{\\text{min}} \\times \\frac{2\\pi \\ \\text{rad}}{1 \\ \\text{rev}} \\times \\frac{1 \\ \\text{min}}{60 \\ \\text{s}} = 62.83 \\text{ rad/s}\n$$\n\n**Step 2: Tangential (rim) speed.**\n\n$$\nv = r\\omega = (0.35)(62.83) = \\boxed{21.99 \\text{ m/s}}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Angular acceleration during spin-up.**\n\n$$\n\\alpha = \\frac{\\Delta\\omega}{\\Delta t} = \\frac{62.83 - 0}{4.0} = 15.71 \\text{ rad/s}^2\n$$\n\n**Step 4: Component accelerations of a rim point.**\n\n$$\na_t = r\\alpha = (0.35)(15.71) = 5.50 \\text{ m/s}^2\n$$\n\n$$\na_n = r\\omega^2 = (0.35)(62.83)^2 = 1381.7 \\text{ m/s}^2\n$$\n\n**Step 5: Total acceleration.**\n\n$$\na = \\sqrt{a_t^2 + a_n^2} = \\sqrt{5.50^2 + 1381.7^2} = \\boxed{1381.8 \\text{ m/s}^2}\n$$\n\nThe normal term overwhelms the tangential term by a factor of about 250. A rim point is being yanked inward far harder than it is being sped up, which is why the bonding and reinforcement of high-speed wheels is governed almost entirely by centripetal (bursting) demand.\n\nStep through the grinding-wheel analysis and predict each result before it is revealed. The surprise at the end is how badly the normal term dominates once the wheel reaches speed.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Acceleration of a rim point on the grinding wheel (Example 2.3)\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The wheel reaches 600 rpm. Convert to rad/s. Which is correct?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"62.83 rad/s\",\"600 rad/s\",\"10 rad/s\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"omega = 600 * (2*pi)/60 = 62.83 rad/s. Always convert rev/min to rad/s before using v = r omega.\"},{\"prompt\":\"With r = 0.35 m, what is the tangential (rim) speed v = r omega?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"21.99 m/s\",\"62.83 m/s\",\"0.35 m/s\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"v = (0.35)(62.83) = 21.99 m/s. Every point on the rim shares omega, but linear speed scales with radius.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Spin-up took 4.0 s from rest, so alpha = 15.71 rad/s^2. Which pair of component accelerations is right at full speed?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"a_t = 5.50 m/s^2 and a_n = 1381.7 m/s^2\",\"a_t = 1381.7 m/s^2 and a_n = 5.50 m/s^2\",\"a_t = 21.99 m/s^2 and a_n = 62.83 m/s^2\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"a_t = r alpha = (0.35)(15.71) = 5.50 m/s^2; a_n = r omega^2 = (0.35)(62.83)^2 = 1381.7 m/s^2. The normal term is the runaway one.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Total a = sqrt(a_t^2 + a_n^2) = sqrt(5.50^2 + 1381.7^2) = 1381.8 m/s^2. The normal term beats the tangential by about 250x, so bursting (centripetal) demand, not spin-up, governs the structural design.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- **Confusing force with torque:** a large force applied directly through the pivot can produce almost no rotation.\n- **Ignoring direction:** clockwise and counterclockwise torques must be signed consistently.\n- **Forgetting radius effects:** points on the same rigid body do not all move at the same linear speed during rotation.\n- **Dropping the normal acceleration:** at high $\\omega$, $a_n = r\\omega^2$ usually dwarfs $a_t = r\\alpha$.\n\n<torque-diagram></torque-diagram>\n\n## From Biology to Machines\n\nRigid-body reasoning becomes especially useful in biomechanics and bio-inspired design.\n\n- A cheetah's leg does not simply \"push harder\"; it uses joint geometry and tendon routing to generate favorable torques during stance.\n- Human elbows and knees use changing moment arms through the range of motion, which is why muscle leverage is not constant.\n- Robotic limbs often borrow these ideas by shaping linkage geometry so that torque output is highest where support demand is highest.\n\nThis is one reason mechanics and biology talk to each other so naturally: both care deeply about how force, geometry, and motion interact.\n\n## How These Ideas Connect\n\nRigid-body motion ties together five ideas. Select a node to see how torque, angular velocity, and joint geometry hang off the rigid-body model and feed the kinematics you already know.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"rigid_body\",\"torque\",\"angular_velocity\",\"kinematics\",\"joints\"]' title=\"Rigid Body Mechanics\"></concept-map>\n\n---\n\n## Engineering Applications\n\n| Application | Why Rigid-Body Motion Matters |\n| --- | --- |\n| Powered exoskeletons | Joint torque from moment-arm geometry must match human strength needs |\n| Wind turbine rotors | Blade-tip normal acceleration ($r\\omega^2$) drives structural design |\n| Robotic arms | Each joint torque depends on link length and payload position |\n| Wrenches and torque tools | Calibrated handle length sets the achievable bolt torque |\n| Flywheel energy storage | Burst safety is governed by rim centripetal loading |\n\n> **Engineering in Action:** The same $\\tau = Fd$ logic that sizes a wrench also sizes the actuator in a surgical robot and the gearbox in a wind turbine. Only the numbers change; the geometry of turning effect is universal.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n### Key Equations\n\n| Concept | Equation |\n| --- | --- |\n| Tangential speed | $v = r\\omega$ |\n| Tangential acceleration | $a_t = r\\alpha$ |\n| Normal acceleration | $a_n = r\\omega^2 = v^2/r$ |\n| Total point acceleration | $a = \\sqrt{a_t^2 + a_n^2}$ |\n| Torque (vector) | $\\vec{\\tau} = \\vec{r} \\times \\vec{F}$ |\n| Torque (magnitude) | $\\lvert\\tau\\rvert = rF\\sin\\theta = Fd$ |\n\n### Key Takeaways\n\n- A rigid body keeps every internal distance fixed, so we track both translation and rotation.\n- Angular velocity is shared by all points; tangential speed grows with radius.\n- Torque depends on force, distance, and angle, and is maximized by a perpendicular pull.\n- The moment arm $d = r\\sin\\theta$ is the cleanest way to think about turning effect.\n- At high spin, normal acceleration dominates and governs structural safety.\n\n---\n\n<dynamic-scenario topic=\"Robotic Knee Torque\" context=\"A biomechatronics team is redesigning a powered knee brace and must decide whether to lengthen the actuator moment arm or increase motor force to help users stand up from a chair.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"A technician pushes on a stuck door very hard, but they push almost directly at the hinge. Why does the door barely rotate?\"\n  conceptId=\"torque\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"Because torque depends on both force and moment arm, and the moment arm is nearly zero near the hinge.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"Because all horizontal forces automatically cancel when an object is rigid.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"Because rigid bodies cannot rotate unless they are already moving.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Correct. A force applied close to the pivot has very little lever arm, so it creates very little torque even if the force magnitude is large.\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"Two points lie on the same spinning wheel. Point A is twice as far from the center as Point B. If the wheel spins with the same angular velocity everywhere, how do their tangential speeds compare?\"\n  conceptId=\"angular_velocity\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"Point A moves twice as fast as Point B because tangential speed scales with radius.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"They move at the same tangential speed because all points on a rigid body share the same velocity.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"Point B moves faster because it is closer to the axis and experiences less inertia.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Correct. In rigid-body rotation, angular velocity is shared, but tangential speed depends on distance from the axis through v = r omega.\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Looking Ahead\n\nOnce rotation is part of the story, we can no longer treat motion as a single number along a line. The next step is to compare what different observers see when both they and the object are moving, which leads naturally into **relative motion**.\n\n## References\n\n- OpenStax. (2016). *University Physics, Volume 1.* Sections on Newton’s laws, work and energy, momentum, and rotational dynamics. https://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n- OpenStax. (2016). *University Physics, Volume 1.* Chapter 12: Static Equilibrium and Elasticity. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/12-introduction\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"curvilinear_concept_1","type":"multiple_choice","stem":"A car moves at constant speed around a circular track. What can we say about its acceleration?","options":["Acceleration is zero","Acceleration is tangential only","Acceleration is normal (centripetal) only","Acceleration has both components"],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Constant speed means aₜ = 0. But direction changes, so aₙ = v²/r ≠ 0.","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["kinematics","angular_velocity"],"learning_objective_ids":["dynamics-01-02-lo-02"]},{"id":"curvilinear_concept_2","type":"multiple_choice","stem":"In which direction does the normal acceleration point?","options":["Along velocity","Opposite to velocity","Toward center of curvature","Away from center"],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Normal (centripetal) acceleration always points toward the center of curvature.","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["kinematics"],"learning_objective_ids":["dynamics-01-02-lo-02"]},{"id":"curvilinear_numeric_1","type":"numeric","stem":"A particle moves at 15 m/s on a curve of radius 50 m. Find the normal acceleration.","final_answer":{"value":4.5,"unit":"m/s²","tolerance":0.1},"difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["kinematics","angular_velocity"],"learning_objective_ids":["dynamics-01-02-lo-02"]},{"id":"curvilinear_numeric_2","type":"numeric","stem":"A car has aₜ = 3 m/s² and aₙ = 4 m/s². Find the magnitude of total acceleration.","final_answer":{"value":5,"unit":"m/s²","tolerance":0.1},"difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["kinematics"],"learning_objective_ids":["dynamics-01-02-lo-02"]},{"id":"curvilinear_circular","type":"numeric","stem":"A wheel rotates at 120 rpm. Find the angular velocity in rad/s.","hints":["1 rpm = 2π/60 rad/s"],"final_answer":{"value":12.57,"unit":"rad/s","tolerance":0.1},"difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["angular_velocity"],"learning_objective_ids":["dynamics-01-02-lo-02"]},{"id":"dynamics_01_02_lo_01_direct_check","type":"conceptual","stem":"Provide a concise response that demonstrates this learning objective: Calculate torque at rotational joints.","expected_answer":"The response accurately demonstrates the section objective: Calculate torque at rotational joints, using relevant section concepts, evidence, or calculations.","explanation":"This direct-evidence item is used when no closed-response item adequately samples the objective. Score it with the section concepts and worked examples as the rubric.","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["dynamics-01-02-lo-01"]},{"id":"dynamics_01_02_lo_03_direct_check","type":"conceptual","stem":"Provide a concise response that demonstrates this learning objective: Apply standard kinematic formulas to complex bodies.","expected_answer":"The response accurately demonstrates the section objective: Apply standard kinematic formulas to complex bodies, using relevant section concepts, evidence, or calculations.","explanation":"This direct-evidence item is used when no closed-response item adequately samples the objective. Score it with the section concepts and worked examples as the rubric.","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["dynamics-01-02-lo-03"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"torque-near-hinge","trigger":"Students often import straight-line force intuition into rotational problems and ignore geometry.","pattern":"A large force always creates large rotation, even when it is applied right next to the pivot.","description":"Torque depends on both force magnitude and moment arm. A large force with almost no lever arm can produce very little rotation.","feedback":"Compare pushing a door near the hinge versus at the handle. Which case has the larger turning effect?","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"same-linear-speed","trigger":"Learners confuse shared angular motion with shared linear motion.","pattern":"All points on a rigid body move with the same linear speed during rotation because the body is rigid.","description":"Rigid bodies share angular velocity, not tangential speed. Linear speed depends on radius through v = r omega.","feedback":"On a spinning wheel, does the rim or the hub cover more distance in one second?","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"torque-is-force","trigger":"Both are associated with pushes and pulls, so the distinction gets blurred.","pattern":"Torque is just another word for force.","description":"Force causes translation, while torque measures the turning tendency of a force about an axis.","feedback":"What changes if the same force is applied with a longer wrench?","rail_action":"ask"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"5c7c7e6f3333db376ec03b3d6b3111a8d42dcdc7b870d36dad72170693e860c4","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}